er seven governorships is  so important because this is now seven  more states that could decide to follow  the Paris climate guidelines even if  Donald Trump calls the United States out  of that agreement at the national level  this is seven more states that can  legalize marijuana as some states did  and restore voting rights to felons as  some states did or protect workers  rights which Scott Walker was not doing  in Wisconsin quite the opposite so that  is really really big and then the other  good news from yesterday is that if you  look at some specific states you see  that trumpism destroyed them and I want  to go back to Kansas the very red state  of Kansas where Donald Trump defeated  Hillary Clinton by 20 points in 2016 56  to 36 they have a Democrat who is going  to be governor defeating Trump crony  Kris Kobach Democrats in Kansas also  took one of four House seats from an  incumbent Republican and the person who  took it is Cherise David's which is huge  so in Kansas we've really seen how  bringing in this cut taxes and defund  social programs Sam Brownback  right-winger ISM trumpism I don't know  what name you want to assign to it it  led to a definitive leftward move for  Kansas that is really great news and  then also good news are the ballot  initiatives Florida banned offshore  drilling and banned indoor vaping they  restored voting rights for felons  Michigan legalized marijuana by popular  vote they will be the tenth state to  legalize marijuana that's a clear trend  nationally and it's of course going to  continue the momentum continues to build  so listen the big picture is Democrats  took the house they didn't lose that  much in the Senate given how the races  favored Republicans they gained seven  governorships and a bunch of different  progressive ballot initiatives were  passed we won the popular vote in Senate  races by 12 million votes if you  count for two Democrats running in  California it's more like nine and a  half million votes and won the popular  vote overall if you look at the popular  vote across everything that happened  yesterday at the net you know Governor's  House and Senate Democrats won the  popular vote by about eight percentage  points that's a blow out okay in  American politics that is a blow out by  any standard so Republicans do need to  be very worried there's all the positive  stuff okay that that we're getting out  of the way but here's where it starts to  get very very ugly  first of all does taking over the house  and plus seven governors do a damn thing  about turning around the corporate  takeover  of our political system and our economy  for the last 40 years the answer is it  doesn't do a thing and it's not fair to  expect that you would change that with  one election and I know that we can say  well there have been some further  leftists that won in the house AOC in  New York and some others that's fine but  only if they're able to achieve change  is that going to be a success point so  number one when I see the most  destructive and ignorant president in  decades single-handedly destroying  important geopolitical alliances around  the world making the United States look  like a joke insulting everyone making  economic policy with the total lack of  understanding of the consequences of  that policy and Democrats barely take  over the house I'm not just blaming  voters I'm not just blaming the  Democratic Party but we've got to get it  together okay if you care about what's  going on you have to vote and I know  voter turnout was up I know that it was  but it's got to be up more because  otherwise we're getting crushed in 2020  okay if there was ever a time to vote  wasn't it yesterday and meanwhile it's  great that Democrats took over the house  and gained seven seats in terms of  governor's but there is still no  cohesive Democratic strategy there is  this jumping  back and forth between playing  Republican light and being more  progressive being the sane centrist and  being Republican light in this constant  revolving door and most importantly  because it's okay to run races  differently based on where you're  running that's not the issue the issue  is that Democrats big-picture with  exceptions are not speaking to voters in  a way that truly resonates in terms of  why shouldn't they be afraid of losing  their status perceived or real if they  support Democrats that was one of the  top motivating factors for people voting  for Donald Trump the fear of losing the  status they either perceive they have  actually have or think maybe they would  have at some point in the future I'm not  saying that Democrats need to run the  exact way that Republicans run because  then we're just Republicans that's not  the point I'm making but if so many  people you have the right policies  within a reasonable sort of Overton  Window if you are a Democrat on many  issues but the voters aren't  understanding that and that's a  communication issue fundamentally and  then also they ran a lot of crappy  campaigns how can the Democratic Party  expect to win when this is what they're  able to do after two years of Trump and  some of the worst governance that we've  ever seen I am not one to defend Michael  a vanadis political approach I have my  problems with them but the one area  where I do agree with avenatti is that  he says we actually need to fight and  that doesn't mean compromise our  principles it doesn't mean do things  that we find immoral but Trump will  crush us in 2020 if we continue the  waterfalls and Ginger Snaps approach  that too many but not all Democrats take  you can't be Republican light you can't  pretend that you don't need to play the  game period and if you are disappointed  and you should be to some degree even  though there were victories last night  we have a new project we have two years  now where the goal is clear take over  the white house take over the Senate  expand control of the house and get a  majority in terms of governorships and  I'm going to be talking about that  shortly today's program brought to you  in part by Blink accom slash Pakman  blinka is a great service that i use all  the time what they do is they take  thousands of the most popular nonfiction  books including the newest bestsellers  they condense them into 15-minute  audiobooks that you can listen to and  you still get the most crucial  information and insights from each book  and I've listened to a ton of books on  blink Asst I recently checked out  soccer na mcc's by Simon Cooper we do  have after all the World Cup going on  recently we also have been talking about  BS jobs and there is a great new book BS  jobs by David Graeber which I listened  to on blinka they're constantly adding  new books to the platform you will never  run out of fascinating and critically  acclaimed books to enjoy and our  audience can get a seven day free trial  by going to blink accom slash Pakman if  you're watching on YouTube I'm putting a  link in the video description and after  the free trial if you'd like it  you can continue enjoying thousands of  condensed audiobooks for about five  bucks a month that's be li NK is t.com  /p a que mas
 

